{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Residual risk — dispositions\n\nThree fragilities were observed with citations during this investigation and none is left\nundispositioned. All are **ticket filed**, all linked `discovered_from` this bug:\n\n- **`spongy-illjudged-terrier` (569c-931f-69a2-4c1d)** — `enrich()` builds an unbounded\n  prompt, so the three largest tickets fail with a 400 (`206826 tokens > 200000 maximum`) and\n  cycle claimed -> lease-expired -> pending forever. This is the reason this store's queue is\n  still not quiet after the cwd fix, and the reason the\n  `enrich drain gate check took ~Nms` warning is still firing here (observed live at 429.6 ms\n  and 648.5 ms on two ordinary writes while filing these tickets).\n- **`nuclear-calm-heron` (da68-fc7c-068c-4c53)** — from a worktree the tracker resolves to the\n  WORKTREE path, so `_rebar_dir` puts the drain advisory lock in the worktree's own\n  `.rebar`. Two agents in two worktrees therefore hold two different locks while draining the\n  SAME store, which defeats the lock's only purpose. It also explains why the canonical drain\n  log holds only four tracebacks for a condition that recurs by design — most of the evidence\n  was deleted with the worktrees that produced it, and it accounts for the one log line the\n  cwd fix does NOT explain (`Error: cannot list '<worktree>/.tickets-tracker'`).\n- **`afeard-technical-kodiakbear` (2dc4-9bcd-75b9-4544)** — the three detach sites are\n  copy-paste siblings by their own admission, which is precisely how this one omission reached\n  three files across three tickets over two months. One `spawn_detached()` so the next\n  property is got right once.\n\n**Accepted, not filed:** `_config_sources.repo_root`'s `Path.cwd()` fallback still raises. I\nleft it deliberately — a silently-wrong repo root is worse than a loud failure, and the three\ndetached children no longer reach it now that each gets an explicit cwd. The remaining\nexposure is long-lived IN-PROCESS work, which cannot be given its own cwd; that question is\ncarried in `afeard-technical-kodiakbear`'s scope rather than answered here.\n\n**Gerrit:** change 1974."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787268358466008001,"uuid":"71216bd5-fa77-430a-aa84-eee9191c394a"}